course: dictionary
lesson: 662
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives bailiff three meanings, and the context (the words around it) tells you which one the writer means. «Bailiffs can collect money owed only after a court decision and many warnings, under strict rules, and cannot take essentials.» The words money owed show this bailiff is about unpaid debts.
explain: Read the clues. In a sentence about debts, a bailiff is an official who can take goods from a home. In a TV court scene, when the bailiff says «All rise», the bailiff is the person who keeps order in the courtroom. In a history lesson, a bailiff is a man who managed a lord's land and the farm workers.
explain: Test your choice. Put the meaning into the sentence in place of the word. If «an official who keeps order in a courtroom» still makes sense in «the bailiffs came because the family owed money», then you have chosen the wrong meaning. Choose the meaning that fits the clues best.
fact: A bailiff is a noun. The plural is bailiffs.
fact: Meaning 1 (UK law): an official with legal power to take goods from someone's home, or make them leave, when they have not paid money they owe.
fact: Meaning 2 (US law): an officer who keeps order in a courtroom.
fact: Meaning 3 (history): in old times, a man who managed a lord's land and farm workers.
fact: Phrases: «court bailiff», «the bailiffs came», «send in the bailiffs».
exercise: «Bailiffs can collect money owed only after a court decision and many warnings, under strict rules.» In this sentence, what does a bailiff do? | Keeps the horses in the castle stables | *Collects money that is owed, by strict legal rules | Looks after a lord's farm and workers | Sings in the court choir
exercise: On a TV court show, the bailiff says «All rise». What is the bailiff's job there? | Sells tickets for the trial | Runs a farm for a lord | *Keeps order in the courtroom
exercise: In a history lesson about medieval times, what was a bailiff? | A knight who fought in battles | A man who sold bread in the market | *A man who managed a lord's land and farm workers
test: Read «The bailiffs came to the house because the family owed money.» Which meaning fits best? | *Meaning 1: an official who takes goods for unpaid money | A wee dog, like a terrier | Meaning 2: an officer who keeps order in a courtroom | Meaning 3: a man who managed a lord's farm, in old times
test: Which clue in «Bailiffs can collect money owed only after a court decision» tells you the bailiff is about debt? | medieval | *money owed | courtroom order | farm workers
exam: In «send in the bailiffs» to a family who owes money, which meaning is right? | A type of bird with long legs | A man who managed a lord's land | An officer who keeps order in a court | *An official who takes goods to pay what is owed
